.TH CCOL_MEMPOOL_FREE_ENTRY 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_mempool_free_entry \- free an entry back to the pool
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

ccol_mempool_free_entry(mp, entry);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Puts an allocated entry back on the pool's free list and sets the
caller's pointer to NULL.

The caller names the pool, and the macro does not find it from the
entry. Free an entry to the pool it came from: passing a different pool
is a caller error, and it is fatal.

The pool decides from the address alone whether the entry is its own
before it reads anything through it. An entry inside the pool's buffer
is checked in the pool's status array. For any other address the pool
looks in its record of the entries that its dynamic fallback handed
out and that are not freed yet. An entry of another pool, an address
the pool never handed out and a fallback entry that is already free
all end in the assert, and none of them is read.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
mp     Pool that the entry came from
entry  Entry to free. The macro sets it to NULL afterward
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
O(1) complexity
Calling the macro with a NULL entry is safe. A NULL pool is a caller
error, and it is fatal
The macro detects corruption with assertions

On a thread-safe pool the entry goes into a small cache of the calling
thread, and the shared free list is reached only when that cache is
full. A pool too small to keep a cache (fewer than 8 elements) takes
the pool's lock on every call instead, and so does a thread that the
pool has no cache left for; that is one lock for each call, as on a
pool without a cache. ccol_mempool_create(3) explains how to size a
pool.

The cache makes no difference to the detection of corruption or of a
double free: an entry in a cache is marked free, so a second free of
it is caught exactly as it would be without the cache. The one
exception is two threads that free the same entry at the same instant.
The double-free check runs outside the lock, so one of the two threads
can miss the other's mark. Such a call is a data race in the caller's
program anyway, and a general purpose allocator with a thread cache
has the same limit.

The macro evaluates each argument exactly once. entry must be a
modifiable lvalue, such as a variable or an element of an array, so
with an expression that has a side effect, such as entries[i++], it
frees and clears one element.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Thread-safe if the pool was created with single_threaded = false.
Concurrent callers don't contend on the common path, and an entry that
one thread freed can be handed out to another thread.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_mempool_alloc_entry (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_create (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_free_entry (3)
